<section class="phero"><img class="phero-img" src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><span aria-current="page">Questions about appraisal</span></nav><p class="tag">Answers by topic</p><h1>Questions About Insurance Appraisal in Nebraska</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers grouped by topic: the basics, costs, timing, Nebraska specifics and working with us.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn line-light" href="/free-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><nav class="toc" aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#q-0">Appraisal basics</a><a href="#q-1">Costs and fees</a><a href="#q-2">Timing and deadlines</a><a href="#q-3">Nebraska specifics</a><a href="#q-4">Working with us</a></nav><h2 id="q-0">Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>A process in most property policies for resolving disputes about the amount of loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and two of the three signing an award sets the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal the same as arbitration?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. Appraisal is narrower. It values the loss and does not decide coverage or legal claims.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Usually either the policyholder or the insurer, by written demand, if the policy contains an appraisal clause.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal decide whether damage is covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. Coverage is decided by the carrier and, if necessary, a court. Appraisal values covered damage.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-1">Costs and fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How much does an appraiser cost?</summary><div class="a"><p>Fees vary. Some appraisers charge a flat fee, some hourly and some a percentage of the increase. We put our fee in writing before work starts.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most policies split the umpire’s fee equally between you and the insurer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the first conversation free?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. We review your situation at no cost and tell you whether appraisal is likely to make sense.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can fees be more than the recovery?</summary><div class="a"><p>On a small gap, yes. That is why we recommend running the numbers first.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-2">Timing and deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?</summary><div class="a"><p>Often six to ten weeks for a simple residential claim once both appraisers are named. Larger claims take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to demand appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Some policies set one; many tie it to other conditions. Read your policy and watch the suit-limitation period.</p></div></details><details><summary>How soon must I report hail damage?</summary><div class="a"><p>Policies require prompt notice and some set a specific window for wind and hail. Report as soon as you reasonably can.</p></div></details><details><summary>What if my policy deadline is close?</summary><div class="a"><p>Talk to a Nebraska attorney right away. Appraisal does not necessarily stop the clock on a lawsuit deadline.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-3">Nebraska specifics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in Nebraska?</summary><div class="a"><p>The Nebraska Department of Insurance in Lincoln. It accepts consumer complaints and oversees claim practices.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does Nebraska have a FAIR plan or wind pool?</summary><div class="a"><p>There is no coastal wind pool, and as of this writing Nebraska is not among the states with a FAIR plan. Ask the Department of Insurance about current options if you are nonrenewed.</p></div></details><details><summary>Are percentage hail deductibles common here?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Many Nebraska homeowners policies apply a wind/hail deductible as a percentage of the dwelling limit.</p></div></details><details><summary>What about bad faith?</summary><div class="a"><p>Nebraska law addresses unfair claim practices and recognizes bad-faith claims in some circumstances. That is a legal matter for an attorney, not an appraiser.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="q-4">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are you public adjusters or attorneys?</summary><div class="a"><p>Neither. We provide appraisal, estimating and umpire services. We do not give legal advice or negotiate claims outside appraisal.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you serve all of Nebraska?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes, all 93 counties, from Omaha and Lincoln to the Sandhills and the Panhandle.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I send for a review?</summary><div class="a"><p>Your declarations page, the carrier’s full estimate, any letters, photos and your contractor’s estimate if you have one.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you guarantee results?</summary><div class="a"><p>No honest appraiser can. We give you a candid assessment and a defensible valuation.</p></div></details></div></article>
